  "image_name": "3d8296dff154ee827afce7f42ad97c75.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a chemotherapy-treated tumor cell can become immunogenic over time and influence dendritic cells. A vertical timeline shows the sequence after exposure to doxorubicin. First, the drug induces tumor cell stress and death. Early on, calreticulin (CRT) moves from inside the cell to the cell surface, where it appears to act as an “eat me” signal that promotes uptake by immature dendritic cells. As the stressed cell progresses toward death, HSP90 is activated and more CRT is displayed.\n\nLater, the dying tumor cell releases HMGB1, which is shown interacting with TLR4 on dendritic cells and supporting antigen processing and presentation, leading to dendritic-cell maturation. Additional soluble danger signals, such as ATP and possibly IL-1β, uric acid, and type I interferons, are indicated as further promoting maturation and migration, with MHC-bearing mature dendritic cells as the outcome.\n\nAt the end of the timeline, fragmented tumor remnants are associated with release of “tolerance signals,” and the figure suggests this may instead drive immunosuppression or a tolerogenic dendritic-cell state. Overall, the image highlights that the immune effect of tumor cell death depends on the timing and type of signals released.",
